<header><p class="st-kicker">republic · Caucasus</p><h1 class="st-title">Georgian SSR</h1><p class="st-meta">1921 CE – 1991 CE · capital Tbilisi</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Soviet Georgia from 1921 to 1991, homeland of Stalin and Beria, known for its wine, resorts and strong national sentiment within the USSR.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>In February 1921 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-army">Red Army</a> invaded the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/democratic-republic-of-georgia">Democratic Republic of Georgia</a> and took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tbilisi">Tiflis</a>, and the Georgian SSR was proclaimed. It joined the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/transcaucasian-sfsr">Transcaucasian SFSR</a> in 1922 and became a full union republic in 1936, renaming its capital Tbilisi. A national uprising in August 1924 was crushed with thousands of executions.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/georgia">Georgia</a> was governed by its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/communist-party-of-the-soviet-union">Communist Party</a> within the Soviet system, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/joseph-stalin">Stalin</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lavrentiy-beria">Beria</a> rising from its ranks. Collectivisation and purges hit hard, but the republic prospered from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tea">tea</a>, citrus, wine and tourism. National feeling surfaced in the 1956 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tbilisi">Tbilisi</a> protests and the 1978 defence of Georgian as official language. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eduard-shevardnadze">Eduard Shevardnadze</a> led the party from 1972 to 1985.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>On 9 April 1989 Soviet troops killed peaceful protesters in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tbilisi">Tbilisi</a>, fuelling the independence movement. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zviad-gamsakhurdia">Zviad Gamsakhurdia's</a> nationalists won elections in 1990, and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/georgia">Georgia</a> declared independence on 9 April 1991, becoming the Republic of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgia">Georgia</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/georgian-ssr.jpg" alt="Georgian SSR" width="320" height="160"><figcaption>Image: Dbenbenn, Nokka · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_the_Georgian_Soviet_Socialist_Republic_(1951%E2%80%931990).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1921 CE – 1991 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>republic</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Tbilisi</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Vasil Mzhavanadze, Eduard Shevardnadze, Zviad Gamsakhurdia</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Georgian Orthodox Christianity (officially atheist state)</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Georgian; Russian</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/georgia">Georgia</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Georgian Soviet Socialist Republic, Soviet Georgia</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1956&amp;focus=georgian-ssr" aria-label="Georgian SSR on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5771 691 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Georgian SSR lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M6091 947L6090 947M6091 947L6090 947M6091 947L5820 533M6091 947L5990 907M6091 947L6091 947"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M6090 947h0M6090 947h0M5820 533h0M5990 907h0M6091 947h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6091 947h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6091 947h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Tbilisi. <a href="/atlas?year=1956&amp;focus=georgian-ssr">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
